# Explore Backward Search in Cops and Robbers

A pursuit game becomes a finite calculation when we reason backward from capture. Start with the narrative demonstration below, then open the deeper sections only when you want the full machinery. Background links: [Cops and Robbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cops_and_robbers_(graph_theory)) and [graph theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_theory).

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## Watch exact examples run

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the backward-attractor definition</summary>

For a set $W$ of certified states, admit $(c,r)$ when capture already holds or when there is one legal simultaneous cops' move $c'$ such that capture is immediate or every legal robber reply $r'$ gives $(c',r')\in W$. The order $\exists c'\, orall r'$ records that the cops move first.

{{algorithm:0}}
</details>

## Why order matters

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the bounded-horizon proof</summary>

At horizon $0$, table membership and immediate capture coincide. Inductively, membership at stage $n+1$ supplies one legal first cops' move whose every noncaptured reply lies at stage $n$; the induction hypothesis supplies a strategy for the remaining $n$ rounds. Reading the same argument backward proves the converse.
</details>

## Growing regions and capture ranks

{{visualization:0}}

A state’s first-entry stage is a progress rank. A recorded witness move makes every noncaptured reply descend to a smaller rank.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal fixed points, minimality, and kernels</summary>

If two consecutive tables agree, the update has reached a fixed point. Any set containing all captured states and closed under the update contains every generated table, by induction and monotonicity. More generally, orienting edges toward strictly lower natural-number rank produces a well-founded relation with a unique kernel.
</details>

## Compare graph families

{{visualization:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal complexity and symmetry reduction</summary>

With $q$ vertices and $k$ labeled cops there are $q^{k+1}$ states. If each closed neighborhood has at most $d$ vertices, a direct update costs $O(q^{k+1}d^{k+1})$. Permutation symmetry suggests replacing $q^k$ cop tuples by $ inom{q+k-1}{k}$ multisets.

{{algorithm:2}}
</details>

